INT. COUNTING HOUSE - SECOND FLOOR - NIGHT

London, 1858. Gaslight, brown and thick. High desks lined with ledgers. A tall window veiled by fog. The smell of ink and coal.

HENRIETTA PLUMB, 40s, a bookkeeper in grey wool, sits upright at a slanted desk, her pen scratching steadily. A pince-nez perches on her nose. Her spine is straight as a rule.

CONSTABLE OAKES, 20s, bulky and young, stands at the door with a lantern, shifting from foot to foot.

A GENTLEMAN, 50s, sits in the visitors' chair with his hat on his knee. Silk cravat, a watch-chain, hands that have never lifted anything heavy.

OAKES
Evening, Mrs. Plumb.

HENRIETTA
Constable.

OAKES
This gentleman says he has an appointment.

HENRIETTA
Does he.

GENTLEMAN
I wrote.

HENRIETTA
I received no letter.

GENTLEMAN
I'm sure it was lost in the fog.

HENRIETTA
Everything is.

She sets down the pen. Squares the ledger. Looks at him for a long time over the pince-nez.

HENRIETTA (CONT'D)
Your name, sir?

GENTLEMAN
Blackwood. Asher Blackwood.

HENRIETTA
And your business?

BLACKWOOD
Your employer owes me a sum.

HENRIETTA
Mr. Tarrant owes many people many sums.

BLACKWOOD
Mine is larger.

HENRIETTA
Then I would like to see your paperwork.

Blackwood draws a folded paper from his coat and sets it on her desk. She unfolds it with two fingers, as if it were a dead moth. Her eyes travel down the page.

OAKES
Is it genuine, ma'am?

HENRIETTA
It is very well forged.

BLACKWOOD
I beg your pardon.

HENRIETTA
Mr. Tarrant never signs with a loop on the T.

Blackwood's smile does not falter, but his hand drifts toward his coat. Oakes lifts the lantern. The light jumps across the ledgers.

OAKES
Sir, I'd keep your hands where I can see them.

BLACKWOOD
Naturally.

He lifts both palms. Henrietta folds the paper in thirds and slides it into a drawer. She locks it.

HENRIETTA
Constable, you may escort Mr. Blackwood to the door.

OAKES
Yes, ma'am.

BLACKWOOD
We'll meet again, Mrs. Plumb.

HENRIETTA
I do hope not. I have the books to close.

They go. The door shuts. She takes up her pen and writes the date at the top of a clean page, and the nib does not tremble once.
